   203	## Secondary: positive-C rank 191, and the exact classical dependence
   204	
   205	`linear-c-transverse-rank-astra` proves `ker(V_C → k[X,W], P ↦ J(P,G)) = k·1`
   206	and hence **full**-C rank 191 at every char-0 base point, unconditionally
   207	(`:88–96`). For the **positive-only** rows the kernel condition weakens to
   208	`J(P,G) = κ ∈ k`, and the report closes `κ = 0` only by assuming the base admits
   209	an actual Keller pair `(F,G)` of degrees (99,66): it sets `Q = P − (κ/j)F`,
   210	uses the centralizer `k[G]`, and derives `deg Q = 99 ∉ 66·Z_{>0}` (`:113–137`).
   211	That is why the statement is currently conditional on a Keller point.
   212	
   213	GGV22 §2 removes the Keller hypothesis from that step. Let `P ∈ V_C` be
   214	nonconstant with `J(P,G) = κ ∈ k^×`. Then `(P,G)` is a Keller pair with
   215	`deg P ≤ 35` (`linear-c…:54`, `factored-jacobian-gate…:71`) and `deg G = 66`, so
   216	`max = 66 < 125` and `{deg P, 66} ≠ {72,108}`. By §2 it is not a counterexample,
   217	hence `K[P,G] = K[x,y]` and `G` is a **coordinate**. Now the classical input,
   218	stated exactly:
   219	
   220	- Jung (1942) / van der Kulk (1953) alone give only `deg P | 66` or `66 | deg P`;
   221	  with `deg P ≤ 35` this permits `deg P ∈ {1,2,3,6,11,22,33}` and **does not
   222	  close the case**. Divisibility is insufficient and must not be cited as if it
   223	  were.
   224	- The operative statement is the top-form corollary: a coordinate of `K[x,y]`
   225	  has one place at infinity, so its leading form is `c·ℓ^{deg}` for a single
   226	  linear form `ℓ`. This is the Abhyankar–Moh embedding theorem
   227	  (*Embeddings of the line in the plane*, Crelle 276 (1975), Moh's `[A-M.2]`,
   228	  Moh p. 212) together with Jung–van der Kulk tameness — the same two references
   229	  Arrow 4 of the charged full-ideal gate already carries, so no new external
   230	  perimeter is opened.
   231	
   232	`G_top = y^{18}(y−x)^{48}` has **two distinct** linear factors, so `G` is not a
   233	coordinate. Contradiction; hence `κ = 0`, hence `P ∈ k·1` by the unconditional
   234	full-C theorem. **Conclusion: positive-C rank 191 at every characteristic-zero
   235	base point of the frozen source**, conditional on GGV22 §2 and on the two
   236	classical results named — and on nothing else, in particular not on the existence
   237	of a Keller point. Note the pleasant asymmetry: the hypothesis “two distinct
   238	leading linear factors” is what does the work, and it is a property our `G` has
   239	*by construction* at every base point, parameter-free.
   240	
   241	Three limits, held deliberately:
   242	
   243	- This is **not an independent proof**. It is an interface: the derivation above
   244	  is three lines, and all of its force is borrowed from GGV22 §2, which this lane
   245	  did not read. It inherits `OPEN[PRIMARY-TEXT-UNREAD]` verbatim.
   246	- **No posterior global `(C,a)` injectivity claim.** The enlarged `(C,a)` block
   247	  is not rescued: at the actual source specialization `D=0, b=0` one has `G=h²`,
   248	  and `P=h` (`Δa=2, ΔC=0`) satisfies `J(h,h²)=0` — a nonconstant element of the
