+
+`PRIMARY99_VERDICT=APPLIES_AS_EXTERNAL_THEOREM`. The local 25-page
+arXiv:2204.14178v1 closes `OPEN[PRIMARY-TEXT-UNREAD]`. Theorem 2.1 says that
+*any* counterexample has maximum degree at least 125 or degree pair
+`(72,108)/(108,72)` (paper pp.2–3; `ggvh-2204.14178v1.txt:94-126`). This is not
+restricted to an arbitrary target representative or stated only for a minimal
+normalized pair.
+
+Actual 99 chain: §2 assigns maximum 99 to `A0=(9,24),(m,n)=(2,3)`.
+Proposition 4.2 (pp.7–10; text `:348-491`) sends any such counterexample to one
+of three nonnegative Newton polygons with `[P,Q]=x`, all sharing P endpoints
+`(6,16),(6,18)` and Q endpoints `(9,24),(9,27)`. Theorem 5.1 (pp.14–20;
+text `:657-981`) forbids polynomial pairs with exactly those endpoint data and
+`[P,Q]=x+g(y)`; take `g=0`. Its proof constructs the approximate root, obtains
+a separable degree-six polynomial, polynomializes the `D_k`, eliminates nine
+coefficient equations to (5.9), then contradicts (5.11) for both multiplicity
+branches `k>=8` and `k<=7`. Corollary 5.7 is the separate `(9,27)`/108 branch,
+not the 99 kill.
